A||N||5||FieroJoe||AJAR light trigger circuit woes||||8||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||03-20-2001||10:24 PM||||*warning, long topic*<P>This whole AJAR mess Ive been working on for too long now has started to get very annoying.  I am installing my alarm, and I want to use the AJAR circuit as the negative trigger for the alarm.  The way the Fiero's wiring works, whenever the hood, decklid, or a door is open, there is ground provided to the AJAR bulb (+12v provided from gauges fuse, hot in run.), thus illuminating it.<P>So, I was planning on splicing into that wire and just using it to be my negative trigger for the alarm.  The alarm will set off once it finds a path to ground through that wire.<P>Ok, I connect that wire to it.  Arm the alarm, and then it does its thing to tell me that there is an open door.  Hmm, nope there isnt.  I reach in and turn the ignition on, no AJAR light coming on.  To make sure that the AJAR circuit is still working, I leave the ignition on and open a door.  It comes on, so I know that is good.<P>After playing around with the wires and the alarm, I just totally disconnected the wire from that splice.  With the ignition off, I test for ground at the AJAR light (btw, it is terminal 11 on the black IP connector [not the one connected to speedo])  Yup, there's ground.  I turn on the ignition, no ground when all doors closed!<P>Ah ha, I think to myself, there is the problem.  The ajar light gets ground when the ignition is off, but no ground when ignition is on, unless a door is open.<P>So what do I do now?  I still want to use that splice into the AJAR ciruit as my negative trigger.  I dont see anything in my manual that shows there is a ignition controlled ground, but I may not be looking in the right place.  Someone please help!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroJoe||03-21-2001||09:35 PM||||,||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||03-22-2001||05:11 PM||||Now let me see if I got this right.<P> 1. The ajar circuit work fine with out any of your connections.<P>2. There is a ground with iignition off and door open.. <P>3. But no ground when ignition is on, unless a door is open. Isn't that how it is suppose to work?<P>4. Are you sure your alarm is negitive triggered?  When the alarm is wired can you measure a voltage at the alarm connection before you connect it to the ajar circuit?<P>5. Try a diode inline with the alarm trigger wire to prevent it from interacting with the ajar circuit.  Does that change anything?<P>Let me know what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||03-22-2001||09:14 PM||||Well, I think the diode thing would have worked, and I was going to try it, but then I just decided that I really didnt care if the ajar light came on when the trunk was open anyway, so I cut the wire that is grounded when the decklid is open (dark green) and just attached the negative hood/trunk trigger wire for my alarm to that and one for the hood.  I then connected the negative trigger wire for the doors to the negative side of the courtesy lights.  It all works fine now, thanks for all your help!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||skitime||03-22-2001||10:11 PM||||Great to hear it is working. So the whole problem was the trunk circuit. Hmmm I will have to think about that.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||03-22-2001||10:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by skitime:<BR><B>Great to hear it is working. So the whole problem was the trunk circuit. Hmmm I will have to think about that.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>well actually the whole reason I had this problem was that I wanted to protect the trunk with the alarm too.  Before that, I had the hood on one of the negative triggers for the alarm, and then the doors on the ctsy lights.  But when I decided I wanted the trunk too, I thought that I could connect everything to the ajar circuit, to simplify things.  But, of course, it only made things more complicated.  oh well, going good now!||||reg||1
